  "query_preview": "../Monday through Friday/...Monday, a surface high pressure\nwill be dominant along the coast, serving as the catalyst for \ncontinued sea breezes for our region; northwest moderate to fresh \nbreezes for our coastal waters and strong breezes impacting \nlocalized areas in the inner channels (Taiya, N/Lynn Canal to name\na few). Monday night a low positions itself in the NW Gulf \nbringing split flow to the coast: southeast winds from Fairweather\nGrounds to Cape St. Elias and continued north-westerlies south of\nCape Edgecumbe. This system will be responsible for bringing \nwidespread rain to areas along and west of Icy Strait, to Prince \nWilliam Sound, further detail on rain at the end. For areas south \nof the majestic Admiralty Island, expecting some cloudiness but we\nwill likely see an extension of summer conditions... temperatures\nin their seasonal normals and 20 percent or less chances for \nrain. One of a precious few remaining opportunities to feel the \nsun before we submit to the winter\u2019s darkness. \n\nTouching on the rain potential, at this time most guidance \nsolutions point to light rainfall for next week; however, there \nare some outliers picking up on much higher IVT magnitudes from an\nexcellent fetch of moisture across the N/Pac, which would \ntranslate to increased chances for more widespread rain. This \nuncertainty is based off additional moisture transport from \ntropical storms/hurricanes in the central Pac. For now, we message\nlighter rain for the north as this moisture plume is sheared \napart Monday into the midweek.",
